    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d706">

                <pb facs="0656" n="649" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg649"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/10896: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-30T13:00:00+08:00">October 30, 1937—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 30—7:28 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>868. Department’s 325, October 29, 1 p.m. German Ambassador has just returned
                    from Shanghai where he went to meet wife and escort her to Nanking. He informs
                    me that he saw Kawagoe and also Ito<anchor n="83" xml:id="d706-f1"/> while
                    there, the latter at an informal tea tendered him and his wife by member of
                    Japanese Embassy. He states that he received no impression that Japanese had
                    formulated any terms, that they merely talked usual generalities. I do not
                    believe that he is acting in capacity described; he seemed very pessimistic. I
                    have just returned from a conversation with Kung<anchor n="84" xml:id="d706-f2"
                    /> who in reply to a question about rumors regarding peace overtures stated that
                    Chinese would not discuss peace terms with Japanese prior to Brussels Conference
                    to which Japanese case has been referred.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d706-f1" n="83" xml:id="d706-f1-content">
                <p>Nobuni Ito, Japanese Minister to Poland until 1937.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d706-f2" n="84" xml:id="d706-f2-content">
                <p>H. H. Kung, Chinese Minister of Finance and Vice President of the Executive Yuan
                    (Vice Premier).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
